  "query_preview": "..\n\nInitial stages of a more significant period of increasing upper \nheights expected over the next 48 hours will occur today, as \nshortwave energy overhead this morning vacates the region. Some \npockets of very light showers or virga evident on radar associated \nwith this wave and accompanying deeper mid level moisture will also \nend. Organizing low pressure system as substantial height falls \noccur across the plains will then draw a warm frontal boundary \nacross the region late today into tonight. Overall, moisture quality \nremains quite limited despite some increase across the lower levels, \nsuggesting simply a lower end potential again for some spotty light \nshowers/sprinkles as broader ascent occurs along the advancing \nfrontal slope. Otherwise, main story early this week remains the \nperpetuation of very mild conditions held under this pattern. The \nelevated mean thickness field combined with a strengthening pattern \nof warm air advection maintains above average temperatures thru at \nleast Wednesday. Warmest conditions expected Tuesday, with a peak in \nthe advective process within deepening southwest flow ensuring 850 \nmb temperatures push into the lower teens. This will bring afternoon \ntemperature to near record levels, with highs solidly in the mid \n70s. Potential for minimum temperature Tuesday night to hold in the \n60s. \n\nClosed upper level circulation will gradually lift into the upper \nMissisippi valley midweek. This will draw an attendant lead cold \nfront through lower Michigan Wednesday evening. Period of stronger \nmoisture transport as greater height falls edge into the region \nahead of the front, along with possible shortwave energy embedded \nwithin the flow, will bring increasing potential for light shower \nproduction during the daylight period. Frontal passage will provide \na secondary focus by late in the day. Outgoing forecast maintains a \nhigh likelihood for light rain, but with rainfall totals generally \nunder quarter of an inch as the best forcing holds to the northwest \nwhile more meaningful instability holds across the Ohio valley. \n\nOccluding low pressure system centered over lake Superior at 12z \nThur morning will continue to weaken as the entire circulation \npivots across the region through Friday. Generally milder conditions \nremain within the immediate post-frontal environment Thursday, \nbefore the cooling trend brings a more seasonable thermal profile by \nFriday and next weekend. General increasing in upward vertical \nmotion as the upper trough pivots through will maintain at least \nlower chances for light rain development during this time.",
